ount":{"login":"NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","providerType":"User"},"email":"info@ntari.org","name":"the Institute"},"authoredDate":"2026-09-13T11:13:00Z","committedDate":"2026-09-13T11:13:00Z","committer":{"account":{"login":"web-flow","providerType":"User"},"email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"observedAt":"2026-10-08T23:54:22.045Z","repositoryId":1311239734,"sha":"b14f760071ee9da4f7c0c5d4a7c9b8c2027520ad"},{"author":{"account":{"login":"NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","providerType":"User"},"email":"info@ntari.org","name":"the Institute"},"authoredDate":"2026-09-03T15:23:45Z","committedDate":"2026-09-03T15:23:45Z","committer":{"account":{"login":"web-flow","providerType":"User"},"email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"observedAt":"2026-10-08T23:54:22.045Z","repositoryId":1311239734,"sha":"477bed56a4ae1d668cd06835df3ff47762c13a00"},{"author":{"account":{"login":"NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","providerType":"User"},"email":"info@ntari.org","name":"the Institute"},"authoredDate":"2026-09-02T15:16:09Z","committedDate":"2026-09-02T15:16:09Z","committer":{"account":{"login":"web-flow","providerType":"User"},"email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"observedAt":"2026-10-08T23:54:22.045Z","repositoryId":1311239734,"sha":"f7264b42839a47725776f16a5b88b19ed37b2119"},{"author":{"account":{"login":"NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","providerType":"User"},"email":"info@ntari.org","name":"the Institute"},"authoredDate":"2026-09-02T14:57:35Z","committedDate":"2026-09-02T14:57:35Z","committer":{"account":{"login":"web-flow","providerType":"User"},"email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"observedAt":"2026-10-08T23:54:22.045Z","repositoryId":1311239734,"sha":"44a08dc5b3d6cbd0dff73ea9a5d4362956dfc4ee"},{"author":{"account":{"login":"NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","providerType":"User"},"email":"info@ntari.org","name":"the Institute"},"authoredDate":"2026-07-24T16:45:10Z","committedDate":"2026-07-24T16:45:10Z","committer":{"account":{"login":"web-flow","providerType":"User"},"email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"observedAt":"2026-10-08T23:54:22.045Z","repositoryId":1311239734,"sha":"d3e2267bdf017085af6901a3ed5aa4029a907cc7"}],"observedAt":"2026-10-08T23:54:22.045Z","raw":[{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/196215170?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","id":196215170,"login":"NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","node_id":"U_kgDOC7IBgg","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/320251986ecbafc1c3259cb9325305f60cb52f01/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-08T15:30:57Z","email":"info@ntari.org","name":"the Institute"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-08T15:30:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"P2-002: add missing translations (14 files), signed (#19)\n\nThe same fourteen files as #17 - README and P1-004 in the seven\nP2-002 languages - recommitted with the sign-off the DCO check\nrequires; #17''s commits lacked it.\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"bd358445a7633074f3ae79537b3e11949581c0a3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/git/trees/bd358445a7633074f3ae79537b3e11949581c0a3"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/git/commits/320251986ecbafc1c3259cb9325305f60cb52f01","verification":{"payload":"tree bd358445a7633074f3ae79537b3e11949581c0a3\nparent d24e404e425481fe5c283dc38e66d7f8c7bee8d5\nauthor the Institute <info@ntari.org> 1791473457 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791473457 -0400\n\nP2-002: add missing translations (14 files), signed (#19)\n\nThe same fourteen files as #17 - README and P1-004 in the seven\nP2-002 languages - recommitted with the sign-off the DCO check\nrequires; #17''s commits lacked it.\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqx7cxCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAzToQAI5qXENo8Igc811BYyWh9DHZ\n+wIIfaA+Y+hWfhNJ4scqwjDqNTPF250h3EfAztEjgd7INjn55ydzS56xea7Ee2Xu\nmfT6G0L3jQ+Z9pEyaqeq95qum1IffSHKVE7LoNnuSWqvk1OTYpl/ZMqQeQxHWXbZ\nmuho2ApkLCaWYnt766itewjSoSHYtJtCXyPDhEIYCx1105NV6/0gKFNHKiH6zExG\nvZHdtJmqvU+3mknNSBnt9tmByWJ75NCoDapK+TecA+eAErb4dFtN9c1oKz684iH7\n3RZSsMtF+NWUWmsmDwRi63HaFhKyf/p8Bb7yhm8k//Jps7xAyk+ncVTkb0NVTLOu\nLMxd4HI8jwFN08AuuBkYvSbN4TNzp43nT8QzlGvsloS8AWmaFHFj1CK+YdCfARsh\nOZfsi2UIJmfFCkpCR9aP8qEuwJXOxj9y5O81CrNYvHU88OMR3PivbpvDHvQpiOPY\n7NTSciMt2l/uFUyxIh9tfQPfnmqyZfVijseTwWdzGZfl3gS96WY75bPTDmI1kc0J\nSu2bQoZY1P8yv0L2BJKN+JAV0iBUvJRystsyJqKthV7xx99eMIjhRNe/vp1dnmFf\n59gI2x+B6yXeOKWfzflDkjVbxnCJpgpOglmV1hc0Urt2p6VN53YLGk01c7qLhhbZ\nRUmoRuX3vAjlbUSjUKLK\n=Mg/4\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-08T15:31:05Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commit/320251986ecbafc1c3259cb9325305f60cb52f01","node_id":"C_kwDOTifuNtoAKDMyMDI1MTk4NmVjYmFmYzFjMzI1OWNiOTMyNTMwNWY2MGNiNTJmMDE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commit/d24e404e425481fe5c283dc38e66d7f8c7bee8d5","sha":"d24e404e425481fe5c283dc38e66d7f8c7bee8d5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/d24e404e425481fe5c283dc38e66d7f8c7bee8d5"}],"sha":"320251986ecbafc1c3259cb9325305f60cb52f01","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/320251986ecbafc1c3259cb9325305f60cb52f01"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/196215170?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","id":196215170,"login":"NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","node_id":"U_kgDOC7IBgg","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/d24e404e425481fe5c283dc38e66d7f8c7bee8d5/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-08T15:30:49Z","email":"info@ntari.org","name":"the Institute"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-08T15:30:49Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Act 1 of the amendment plan: witness draw, ratified switch, integrity posture, companions published (#18)\n\n* Name the exit cost of each layer and how conflict is settled there\n\nThe Institutional Discipline principle stated the cost-of-leaving rule but\nnever said which layer falls into which band, so the rule could not be\napplied by a reader. It now names them: Substrate and E&I are cheap to\nleave, Covenant and Governance are dear, Record is impossible.\n\nEach layer body now carries a paragraph saying what expulsion costs there\nand what that cost forces conflict resolution to look like. This also\nevens out the layer descriptions, which ranged from one clause to three\nparagraphs.\n\nThe Covenant adjudication mechanics move up from the Orchestrator Tier\ninto the layer body, since they describe the layer''s whole conflict\nposture rather than one tier''s job.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Define prosumer up front, in its own opening beat\n\nThe definition was a subordinate clause in the middle of a sentence whose\nmain job was explaining the document''s name, so the term that the whole\narchitecture rests on arrived as an aside and did not stick.\n\nIt now opens the document on its own, and the Janus metaphor follows and\nrefers back to it — the name earns its image from a term the reader\nalready holds, rather than carrying the definition as a parenthetical.\nBolded at first use so it can be found again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Rewrite line 1 to say that every credit is an IOU\n\nReaders were taking line 1 to mean the opposite of what it says: that JFA\nbans IOUs. It is the reverse — every credit in the system is one, created\nwhen two members trade. What is banned is money made any other way, and\nthe line never said so.\n\nThe line now leads with what a credit is, then closes the routes the\nstructure article already rules out: nothing minted, nothing issued from\noutside, nothing accrued as interest.\n\nDeliberately omits \"no reserves,\" which the structure article also lists,\nbecause line 10 requires a deployment to begin collateralized in escrow.\nStating it flatly here would contradict it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Add a plain-language Why This Matters section before the principles\n\nThe document opens with three dense paragraphs carrying Janus, Toffler,\nAcemoglu and Robinson''s corridor, the Red Queen, two NTARI papers and the\nLeviathan in four forms, then goes straight into the principles. A reader\nwho did not follow that theory had nothing to stand on.\n\nThe new section states the argument once, in plain words, with no jargon\nand no citations, and hands off to the principles.\n\nAlso trims a clause from the third introduction paragraph that duplicated\nthe Shared Responsibility principle almost verbatim — both said the\ncommunity that coordinates is the community that checks, and that the two\nare exchanged continuously. The principle keeps it; the paragraph now ends\non the shackled Leviathan, which is what it was building toward.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Write out Economy & Information instead of abbreviating it\n\nThe rule asked for was: expand the term at first use in every paragraph.\nApplied to this document that expands every instance, because 11\nparagraphs use the term and not one uses it twice. The abbreviation was\nnever doing any work.\n\nSo the term is now written out everywhere, and \"(E&I)\" survives only at\nthe definition in the introduction, where it keeps the abbreviation on the\nrecord for the conformance suite, the open questions register and the\ncompanion articles, all of which use it.\n\nThe alternative was repeating \"Economy & Information (E&I)\" eleven times,\nwhich defines an abbreviation the document then never uses.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Write out Economy & Information in the discipline principle\n\nKeeps this branch consistent with #13, which retires the abbreviation from\nthe body of the document. Without it, whichever of the two merges second\nleaves one bare \"E&I\" behind in the principle.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Restore the covenant adjudication wording verbatim\n\nMoving the adjudication mechanics into the layer body, I also reworded\nthem, which broke two registered anchors and failed D4:\n\n  COV-operators-adjudicate  \"platform operators adjudicate between their prosumers\"\n  COV-adjudicators          \"rated on their conduct by both prosumers\"\n\nThese are registered invariants; the phrasing is normative and not mine to\nimprove. The sentences are now moved unaltered, which is what the move was\nsupposed to be. Conformance passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Restore the INTRO-janus anchor phrase verbatim\n\nRewriting the Janus sentence dropped the registered anchor for\nINTRO-janus, \"faces demands for both production and consumption\", and\nfailed D4. The phrase is back verbatim inside the new sentence, which\nstill reads with the definition ahead of it. Conformance passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Cite the issuance model claims the structure article already sources\n\nThe introduction asserts both halves of the issuance argument — exogenous\nchartal money, and endogenous mutual credit — with no citation, while the\nstructure article makes the same two claims and sources them. The document\nis the authoritative instrument, so it should not be the weaker cited of\nthe two.\n\nAdds Knapp (1924) for chartal money and Moore (1988) with Greco (2009) for\nendogenous mutual credit, taken verbatim from the article''s own source\nlist so the two agree.\n\nNo new claims; the sentence says what it said before.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Say what the record layer''s frontend tier actually is\n\nExpanding the abbreviation exposed that this sentence had no clear\ngrammatical reading: \"provided by prosumers on the substrate layer\nEconomy & Information\". It is meant to say the frontend of the record\nlayer is the buying and selling of record storage across the substrate.\n\nThe registered anchor for REC-witness-work, \"compensated compute/record\nservice provided by prosumers\", is normative and survives verbatim; the\ngloss follows it rather than replacing it. Conformance passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Retrack the REC-witness-work anchor and say what the frontend tier is\n\nThe record layer''s frontend tier is the buying and selling of record\nstorage across the substrate. The old sentence never said so, and the\nregistered anchor for REC-witness-work quoted the old wording verbatim, so\nthe sentence could not be fixed without changing the anchor with it.\n\n  was  compensated compute/record service provided by prosumers\n  now  compensating the prosumers who keep the record\n\nThe invariant keeps its ID, section and meaning — record work compensated\nas substrate work. Only the phrase D4 looks for moves, to track where that\nclaim now lives in the sentence. Note carries the dated rewording, as the\nregistry''s other retracked anchors do.\n\nSupersedes the gloss in the previous commit, which preserved the old\nanchor by appending to it rather than replacing it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Section 1 — Name prosumer standing as a path into membership (Governance, orchestrator tier)\n\nApplies Section 1 of the amendment to the official document, 2026-09-22. Proposed, not adopted: §9.2 requires a vote of the delegates and a passing conformance run.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Section 2 — No operator adjudicates a dispute to which it is a party (Covenant layer)\n\nApplies Section 2 of the amendment to the official document, 2026-09-22. Proposed, not adopted: §9.2 requires a vote of the delegates and a passing conformance run.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Section 3 — Register the four invariants the amendment binds (§9.16)\n\nApplies Section 3 of the amendment to the official document, 2026-09-22. Proposed, not adopted: §9.2 requires a vote of the delegates and a passing conformance run.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Add the official-document amendment instrument of 2026-09-22\n\nThe instrument the delegates vote on under §9.2: two substantive sections, the registry changes §9.16 requires in the same act, and an adoption record left blank until the vote.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Phase 2: official document - I-04, I-05, I-17, I-20, I-21, I-24, I-26, I-28, I-30\n\nApplies the September 2026 consistency-review decisions to the\nofficial document (edits J-01 through J-12 of the pass plan). The Lines\nThat Cannot Be Crossed section is byte-identical to the base.\n\nThe conformance suite now fails D4 on two anchors (REC-six-holders,\nCOV-adjudicators) and D6 on the product-name list (LBTAS, named by\nJ-08). Per bylaws 9.16 and the pass plan, neither the registry nor the\nsuite is patched here; proposed changes are in the pull-request report.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Phase 8: open question 11 - record permanence under paid storage\n\nEntered under bylaws 9.3 as raised by the principal on 2026-09-30. The\nopening count moves from four to five open entries.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Phase 7: retrack two anchors and unlist LBTAS as a product name\n\nBy decision of the principal on 2026-09-30, after the consistency pass:\n\n- REC-six-holders: anchor \"held six ways\" -> \"held by six parties\";\n  the note now counts the orchestrator among the holders.\n- COV-adjudicators: anchor \"rated on their conduct by both prosumers\"\n  -> \"the parties to it rate the adjudicator\".\n- LBTAS removed from PRODUCT_NAMES: it is the covenant, not a product,\n  and may be named wherever the Covenant layer label would otherwise be\n  undefined.\n\nIdentifiers and bindings are unchanged; the two anchor changes are a\nregistry amendment under bylaws 9.16 and ride with this amendment act.\nThe suite passes D1-D9 again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Retrack the GOV-prosumer-membership anchor to the consistency-pass wording\n\nThe September 2026 consistency pass (Janus PR #16, item I-05) words the\nprosumer path as \"by participating as a prosumer on a federated\nplatform\", and the bylaws v1.1 draft (bylaws PR #4, 3.1) uses the same\nwords. By decision of the principal on 2026-09-30 the anchor follows\nthat wording; the document sentence and the amendment record are\nchanged to match. Identifier, section and binding are unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Record layer: deletion rule, copy lifetimes, fee split, chain storage funded by the steward\n\nDecisions of the principal, 2026-10-01, following the consistency pass:\n\n- \"Nobody can be deleted from the record\" replaces \"barred\", keeping bar\n  and expulsion to their two meanings.\n- Nothing is erased, but no single copy is the record: each copy lasts\n  only as long as it is kept, and the chain is claimed to outlive the\n  operator only because the substrate, not the operator, stores it.\n- The transport fee is split between the two prosumers home ledgers,\n  each paying in its own unit; the paragraph stops counting spends\n  (\"every spend reverts\").\n- The commitments of every exchange carried by an orchestrator are\n  stored on substrate funded by the stewardship organization.\n\nOpen questions: entry 10 split resolved in draft; entry 11 resolved in\ndraft; new entry 12 (public-chain construction, funding and lifetime;\nfunding resolved, construction open). Suite passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Move federation placement and vote weight out of the official document\n\nBy decision of the principal on 2026-10-01: a prosumer member must choose\nits federation and holds one vote within it, but that rule belongs in the\nbylaws, not in the standard. Section 1 now names only the membership\npath; the two never-issued identifiers GOV-federation-election and\nGOV-one-vote-per-federation are withdrawn from the registry (30 -> 28);\nthe amendment record says so. P1-001 v1.1 (bylaws PR #4) carries the\nelection in 3.9. Suite passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Witnesses rule on a prosumer against its own operator; OQ 12(d) resolved\n\nDecisions of the principal, 2026-10-02:\n\n- A dispute between a prosumer and the operator of its own platform is\n  adjudicated at the witness layer; no operator adjudicates a dispute\n  to which it is a party. The clause matches PR #15 Section 2 word for\n  word, so when the two branches meet the Covenant paragraph resolves by\n  taking this wording. The rater tail now reads \"the operator between\n  its own prosumers, the witnesses otherwise\".\n- Open question 12(d) resolved: commitments of exchanges no orchestrator\n  carries are stored by no one, because a frontend that is not\n  federated is not a member.\n\nSuite passes; registered anchors unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Open questions: entry 7 brought current; new entry 13\n\nUnder bylaws 9.3. Entry 7 still described the v7.1-draft procedure\n(bylaws 2.9, the Vice President delegate, a one-week cooldown); it now\nrecords the v1.1 draft 12.5 procedure as decided on 2026-10-02 and the\nseven procedural points verification left open. New entry 13 records\nthe 2026-10-02 rule that witnesses rule on a prosumer against its own\noperator, and the eight points of procedure it leaves open. Suite\npasses.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Move the amendment instrument to Historical Docs\n\nCONTRIBUTING forbids proposal files beside the standard: the diff is the\nproposal. The instrument stays in the drafting record.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Act 1 of the amendment plan: the document''s share\n\nPer the amendment plan of 2026-10-07, items 1.1, 1.4-1.7, and 1.10:\n\n- Publish the two companions no member could read: the dispute-mechanics\n  design (revised for six holders, the orchestrator, disputes against\n  one''s own operator, and the witness draw) and the structure article\n  that sources prosumer membership (item 1.1).\n- Record layer: witnesses assigned by a draw seeded from the public\n  chain, verifiable by anyone, paid by the substrate market and never\n  by the operator they observe; registered as REC-witness-draw,\n  lineage REC-witness-work (item 1.4).\n- Record layer: witness copies last while their storage is paid, not\n  while the operator pays them (item 1.4).\n- Record layer: delivery is attested by the witnesses alone; the\n  operator keeps its record of a carry and never attests to one;\n  open question 10''s operator slot resolved (item 1.6).\n- Line 10: a switch to full mutual credit is ratified by the\n  deployment''s prosumers; the anchor stands, the enforcement line\n  adds ratification (item 1.5).\n- Substrate protocol tier: integrity-assured, not confidentiality-\n  assured; settlement and record work runs on at least two independent\n  hosts, disagreement rated, never trusted; registered as\n  SUB-redundant-execution; the E&I protocol tier assumes the same;\n  open question 9(c) resolved (item 1.7).\n- Open questions brought current: entries 8, 9, 10, and 13 take the\n  resolutions above; new entries 14 (covenant exit and members''\n  records), 15 (line 9 and capacity, pending Act 2), and 16\n  (counsel item C-9) (item 1.10).\n\nSuite: 30 registered invariants, all executed checks pass.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Register REC-witness-draw and SUB-redundant-execution; L10 adds ratification\n\nA registry change is an amendment of the official document (bylaws 9.16)\nand rides the same act. Both new invariants are implementation-bound and\nreport as delegated and unbound until a repository''s tests cite them.\nSuite: 30 registered invariants, all executed checks pass.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"3ac156c3a12a5413ce56dbf53c2bbbedad437837","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/git/trees/3ac156c3a12a5413ce56dbf53c2bbbedad437837"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/git/commits/d24e404e425481fe5c283dc38e66d7f8c7bee8d5","verification":{"payload":"tree 3ac156c3a12a5413ce56dbf53c2bbbedad437837\nparent fc9feb47d6fa513a06bd3cbeb7b904ee7714b57d\nauthor the Institute <info@ntari.org> 1791473449 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791473449 -0400\n\nAct 1 of the amendment plan: witness draw, ratified switch, integrity posture, companions published (#18)\n\n* Name the exit cost of each layer and how conflict is settled there\n\nThe Institutional Discipline principle stated the cost-of-leaving rule but\nnever said which layer falls into which band, so the rule could not be\napplied by a reader. It now names them: Substrate and E&I are cheap to\nleave, Covenant and Governance are dear, Record is impossible.\n\nEach layer body now carries a paragraph saying what expulsion costs there\nand what that cost forces conflict resolution to look like. This also\nevens out the layer descriptions, which ranged from one clause to three\nparagraphs.\n\nThe Covenant adjudication mechanics move up from the Orchestrator Tier\ninto the layer body, since they describe the layer''s whole conflict\nposture rather than one tier''s job.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Define prosumer up front, in its own opening beat\n\nThe definition was a subordinate clause in the middle of a sentence whose\nmain job was explaining the document''s name, so the term that the whole\narchitecture rests on arrived as an aside and did not stick.\n\nIt now opens the document on its own, and the Janus metaphor follows and\nrefers back to it — the name earns its image from a term the reader\nalready holds, rather than carrying the definition as a parenthetical.\nBolded at first use so it can be found again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Rewrite line 1 to say that every credit is an IOU\n\nReaders were taking line 1 to mean the opposite of what it says: that JFA\nbans IOUs. It is the reverse — every credit in the system is one, created\nwhen two members trade. What is banned is money made any other way, and\nthe line never said so.\n\nThe line now leads with what a credit is, then closes the routes the\nstructure article already rules out: nothing minted, nothing issued from\noutside, nothing accrued as interest.\n\nDeliberately omits \"no reserves,\" which the structure article also lists,\nbecause line 10 requires a deployment to begin collateralized in escrow.\nStating it flatly here would contradict it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Add a plain-language Why This Matters section before the principles\n\nThe document opens with three dense paragraphs carrying Janus, Toffler,\nAcemoglu and Robinson''s corridor, the Red Queen, two NTARI papers and the\nLeviathan in four forms, then goes straight into the principles. A reader\nwho did not follow that theory had nothing to stand on.\n\nThe new section states the argument once, in plain words, with no jargon\nand no citations, and hands off to the principles.\n\nAlso trims a clause from the third introduction paragraph that duplicated\nthe Shared Responsibility principle almost verbatim — both said the\ncommunity that coordinates is the community that checks, and that the two\nare exchanged continuously. The principle keeps it; the paragraph now ends\non the shackled Leviathan, which is what it was building toward.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Write out Economy & Information instead of abbreviating it\n\nThe rule asked for was: expand the term at first use in every paragraph.\nApplied to this document that expands every instance, because 11\nparagraphs use the term and not one uses it twice. The abbreviation was\nnever doing any work.\n\nSo the term is now written out everywhere, and \"(E&I)\" survives only at\nthe definition in the introduction, where it keeps the abbreviation on the\nrecord for the conformance suite, the open questions register and the\ncompanion articles, all of which use it.\n\nThe alternative was repeating \"Economy & Information (E&I)\" eleven times,\nwhich defines an abbreviation the document then never uses.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Write out Economy & Information in the discipline principle\n\nKeeps this branch consistent with #13, which retires the abbreviation from\nthe body of the document. Without it, whichever of the two merges second\nleaves one bare \"E&I\" behind in the principle.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Restore the covenant adjudication wording verbatim\n\nMoving the adjudication mechanics into the layer body, I also reworded\nthem, which broke two registered anchors and failed D4:\n\n  COV-operators-adjudicate  \"platform operators adjudicate between their prosumers\"\n  COV-adjudicators          \"rated on their conduct by both prosumers\"\n\nThese are registered invariants; the phrasing is normative and not mine to\nimprove. The sentences are now moved unaltered, which is what the move was\nsupposed to be. Conformance passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Restore the INTRO-janus anchor phrase verbatim\n\nRewriting the Janus sentence dropped the registered anchor for\nINTRO-janus, \"faces demands for both production and consumption\", and\nfailed D4. The phrase is back verbatim inside the new sentence, which\nstill reads with the definition ahead of it. Conformance passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Cite the issuance model claims the structure article already sources\n\nThe introduction asserts both halves of the issuance argument — exogenous\nchartal money, and endogenous mutual credit — with no citation, while the\nstructure article makes the same two claims and sources them. The document\nis the authoritative instrument, so it should not be the weaker cited of\nthe two.\n\nAdds Knapp (1924) for chartal money and Moore (1988) with Greco (2009) for\nendogenous mutual credit, taken verbatim from the article''s own source\nlist so the two agree.\n\nNo new claims; the sentence says what it said before.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Say what the record layer''s frontend tier actually is\n\nExpanding the abbreviation exposed that this sentence had no clear\ngrammatical reading: \"provided by prosumers on the substrate layer\nEconomy & Information\". It is meant to say the frontend of the record\nlayer is the buying and selling of record storage across the substrate.\n\nThe registered anchor for REC-witness-work, \"compensated compute/record\nservice provided by prosumers\", is normative and survives verbatim; the\ngloss follows it rather than replacing it. Conformance passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Retrack the REC-witness-work anchor and say what the frontend tier is\n\nThe record layer''s frontend tier is the buying and selling of record\nstorage across the substrate. The old sentence never said so, and the\nregistered anchor for REC-witness-work quoted the old wording verbatim, so\nthe sentence could not be fixed without changing the anchor with it.\n\n  was  compensated compute/record service provided by prosumers\n  now  compensating the prosumers who keep the record\n\nThe invariant keeps its ID, section and meaning — record work compensated\nas substrate work. Only the phrase D4 looks for moves, to track where that\nclaim now lives in the sentence. Note carries the dated rewording, as the\nregistry''s other retracked anchors do.\n\nSupersedes the gloss in the previous commit, which preserved the old\nanchor by appending to it rather than replacing it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Section 1 — Name prosumer standing as a path into membership (Governance, orchestrator tier)\n\nApplies Section 1 of the amendment to the official document, 2026-09-22. Proposed, not adopted: §9.2 requires a vote of the delegates and a passing conformance run.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Section 2 — No operator adjudicates a dispute to which it is a party (Covenant layer)\n\nApplies Section 2 of the amendment to the official document, 2026-09-22. Proposed, not adopted: §9.2 requires a vote of the delegates and a passing conformance run.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Section 3 — Register the four invariants the amendment binds (§9.16)\n\nApplies Section 3 of the amendment to the official document, 2026-09-22. Proposed, not adopted: §9.2 requires a vote of the delegates and a passing conformance run.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Add the official-document amendment instrument of 2026-09-22\n\nThe instrument the delegates vote on under §9.2: two substantive sections, the registry changes §9.16 requires in the same act, and an adoption record left blank until the vote.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Phase 2: official document - I-04, I-05, I-17, I-20, I-21, I-24, I-26, I-28, I-30\n\nApplies the September 2026 consistency-review decisions to the\nofficial document (edits J-01 through J-12 of the pass plan). The Lines\nThat Cannot Be Crossed section is byte-identical to the base.\n\nThe conformance suite now fails D4 on two anchors (REC-six-holders,\nCOV-adjudicators) and D6 on the product-name list (LBTAS, named by\nJ-08). Per bylaws 9.16 and the pass plan, neither the registry nor the\nsuite is patched here; proposed changes are in the pull-request report.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Phase 8: open question 11 - record permanence under paid storage\n\nEntered under bylaws 9.3 as raised by the principal on 2026-09-30. The\nopening count moves from four to five open entries.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Phase 7: retrack two anchors and unlist LBTAS as a product name\n\nBy decision of the principal on 2026-09-30, after the consistency pass:\n\n- REC-six-holders: anchor \"held six ways\" -> \"held by six parties\";\n  the note now counts the orchestrator among the holders.\n- COV-adjudicators: anchor \"rated on their conduct by both prosumers\"\n  -> \"the parties to it rate the adjudicator\".\n- LBTAS removed from PRODUCT_NAMES: it is the covenant, not a product,\n  and may be named wherever the Covenant layer label would otherwise be\n  undefined.\n\nIdentifiers and bindings are unchanged; the two anchor changes are a\nregistry amendment under bylaws 9.16 and ride with this amendment act.\nThe suite passes D1-D9 again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Retrack the GOV-prosumer-membership anchor to the consistency-pass wording\n\nThe September 2026 consistency pass (Janus PR #16, item I-05) words the\nprosumer path as \"by participating as a prosumer on a federated\nplatform\", and the bylaws v1.1 draft (bylaws PR #4, 3.1) uses the same\nwords. By decision of the principal on 2026-09-30 the anchor follows\nthat wording; the document sentence and the amendment record are\nchanged to match. Identifier, section and binding are unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Record layer: deletion rule, copy lifetimes, fee split, chain storage funded by the steward\n\nDecisions of the principal, 2026-10-01, following the consistency pass:\n\n- \"Nobody can be deleted from the record\" replaces \"barred\", keeping bar\n  and expulsion to their two meanings.\n- Nothing is erased, but no single copy is the record: each copy lasts\n  only as long as it is kept, and the chain is claimed to outlive the\n  operator only because the substrate, not the operator, stores it.\n- The transport fee is split between the two prosumers home ledgers,\n  each paying in its own unit; the paragraph stops counting spends\n  (\"every spend reverts\").\n- The commitments of every exchange carried by an orchestrator are\n  stored on substrate funded by the stewardship organization.\n\nOpen questions: entry 10 split resolved in draft; entry 11 resolved in\ndraft; new entry 12 (public-chain construction, funding and lifetime;\nfunding resolved, construction open). Suite passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Move federation placement and vote weight out of the official document\n\nBy decision of the principal on 2026-10-01: a prosumer member must choose\nits federation and holds one vote within it, but that rule belongs in the\nbylaws, not in the standard. Section 1 now names only the membership\npath; the two never-issued identifiers GOV-federation-election and\nGOV-one-vote-per-federation are withdrawn from the registry (30 -> 28);\nthe amendment record says so. P1-001 v1.1 (bylaws PR #4) carries the\nelection in 3.9. Suite passes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Witnesses rule on a prosumer against its own operator; OQ 12(d) resolved\n\nDecisions of the principal, 2026-10-02:\n\n- A dispute between a prosumer and the operator of its own platform is\n  adjudicated at the witness layer; no operator adjudicates a dispute\n  to which it is a party. The clause matches PR #15 Section 2 word for\n  word, so when the two branches meet the Covenant paragraph resolves by\n  taking this wording. The rater tail now reads \"the operator between\n  its own prosumers, the witnesses otherwise\".\n- Open question 12(d) resolved: commitments of exchanges no orchestrator\n  carries are stored by no one, because a frontend that is not\n  federated is not a member.\n\nSuite passes; registered anchors unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Open questions: entry 7 brought current; new entry 13\n\nUnder bylaws 9.3. Entry 7 still described the v7.1-draft procedure\n(bylaws 2.9, the Vice President delegate, a one-week cooldown); it now\nrecords the v1.1 draft 12.5 procedure as decided on 2026-10-02 and the\nseven procedural points verification left open. New entry 13 records\nthe 2026-10-02 rule that witnesses rule on a prosumer against its own\noperator, and the eight points of procedure it leaves open. Suite\npasses.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Move the amendment instrument to Historical Docs\n\nCONTRIBUTING forbids proposal files beside the standard: the diff is the\nproposal. The instrument stays in the drafting record.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Act 1 of the amendment plan: the document''s share\n\nPer the amendment plan of 2026-10-07, items 1.1, 1.4-1.7, and 1.10:\n\n- Publish the two companions no member could read: the dispute-mechanics\n  design (revised for six holders, the orchestrator, disputes against\n  one''s own operator, and the witness draw) and the structure article\n  that sources prosumer membership (item 1.1).\n- Record layer: witnesses assigned by a draw seeded from the public\n  chain, verifiable by anyone, paid by the substrate market and never\n  by the operator they observe; registered as REC-witness-draw,\n  lineage REC-witness-work (item 1.4).\n- Record layer: witness copies last while their storage is paid, not\n  while the operator pays them (item 1.4).\n- Record layer: delivery is attested by the witnesses alone; the\n  operator keeps its record of a carry and never attests to one;\n  open question 10''s operator slot resolved (item 1.6).\n- Line 10: a switch to full mutual credit is ratified by the\n  deployment''s prosumers; the anchor stands, the enforcement line\n  adds ratification (item 1.5).\n- Substrate protocol tier: integrity-assured, not confidentiality-\n  assured; settlement and record work runs on at least two independent\n  hosts, disagreement rated, never trusted; registered as\n  SUB-redundant-execution; the E&I protocol tier assumes the same;\n  open question 9(c) resolved (item 1.7).\n- Open questions brought current: entries 8, 9, 10, and 13 take the\n  resolutions above; new entries 14 (covenant exit and members''\n  records), 15 (line 9 and capacity, pending Act 2), and 16\n  (counsel item C-9) (item 1.10).\n\nSuite: 30 registered invariants, all executed checks pass.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n* Register REC-witness-draw and SUB-redundant-execution; L10 adds ratification\n\nA registry change is an amendment of the official document (bylaws 9.16)\nand rides the same act. Both new invariants are implementation-bound and\nreport as delegated and unbound until a repository''s tests cite them.\nSuite: 30 registered invariants, all executed checks pass.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqx7cpCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAN6IQADaBkDAp3LQ+kY5YMiDzhXcw\nMxU/LiVLWcS79ia3zKH62RBsvv8jdLIX70UM6r3609wyq7/wYcQZF1gO8qo/SbZu\nd8na9bOXcK8B3A8PnLamgIvI2WDqK+yjmOjisIhOCzUbGV6HIUiBQhNXmzF1rYm+\nRxzPdmbiysVpCO+5EdwlVN1Vif3wmC2T0uNJkiGy/OutEykFdI56MS0bLsu3of2v\nBRFgUfHDBWOJk0sK4AI/tt6jodLgNDjSyFLKeEmz1w1eplphHPvsGZIcwl0evNbd\n2J9+UcSFbf3jpKmol5g8sNjX1KbryAb6PSuR4o2Hz6pbqNLv39NiZ6oK6RRSFEeD\nYUKUX9y4BPLVah+L/cpY38aGsoQPpYFkPF4zwvBcBMSKBSdlhqXbOqwAxBl+h6Ek\njG27FibKLQbKM92J64lPsHYL/qYu+N/j+B+Np9p/KC2sbwrRhwebBj91qiE5GXGd\nF5E8MwSPoEnG8BvYksQV1otMZv1zVSem2+mEwpL8OMRVkd3iTWJyYgXx3BPP8xhv\nuCW7AuUJCiENAhG3vNsgDgNiZbPqtBYvYPKwHxZlNGSfmWpDKqcyqfhBlj1nHGim\n60K4rA/8Ws6JkGQi1TNmZvn1W+17t4m2w9QUjYNLrFox9QEYXaytUk9oCwg3ReLs\nPYJYQM1IR4pnfFmTCZTE\n=FHLd\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-08T15:30:53Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commit/d24e404e425481fe5c283dc38e66d7f8c7bee8d5","node_id":"C_kwDOTifuNtoAKGQyNGU0MDRlNDI1NDgxZmU1YzI4M2RjMzhlNjZkN2Y4YzdiZWU4ZDU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commit/fc9feb47d6fa513a06bd3cbeb7b904ee7714b57d","sha":"fc9feb47d6fa513a06bd3cbeb7b904ee7714b57d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/fc9feb47d6fa513a06bd3cbeb7b904ee7714b57d"}],"sha":"d24e404e425481fe5c283dc38e66d7f8c7bee8d5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/d24e404e425481fe5c283dc38e66d7f8c7bee8d5"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/196215170?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","id":196215170,"login":"NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","node_id":"U_kgDOC7IBgg","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/fc9feb47d6fa513a06bd3cbeb7b904ee7714b57d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T16:10:25Z","email":"info@ntari.org","name":"the Institute"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T16:10:25Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Carry the residential substrate work into the standard (#8)\n\nThe 2026-09-08 board review raised three physical facts about running\nsubstrate on consumer broadband that the document did not address:\ndynamic addresses behind carrier-grade NAT with acceptable-use policies\nbarring inbound servers, asymmetric and capped uplink, and a host\noperator who can read the memory of a machine they physically possess.\nThat work was done in the document store and never reached this\nrepository, so the standard has been silent on the residential case\nwhile the reference protocol was already built for it.\n\nThe document gains one paragraph in the Substrate protocol tier: nodes\njoin the market over encrypted overlays and outbound polling, without\nrequiring open inbound ports or a static address. This is the\nresidential case of line 11 rather than a new provision - a substrate\nthat ran only where a provider permits inbound service would carry a\nchokepoint at every provider, which line 11 forbids. Registered as\nSUB-no-inbound-requirement, implementation-bound, taking the registry\nfrom 25 invariants to 26 and the README counts with it.\n\nThe seven translations carry the same paragraph and move their source\nsnapshot to 2026-09-08. They do not yet carry the transport fee from\n#6; the snapshot date says so rather than implying coverage they lack.\n\nBandwidth and confidential execution are not answered by the document.\nThey are argued in P1-004, published here because open question 9 links\nto it and the link would otherwise dangle - the same reason the concept\ntriage and prior instruments were published in #1. The paper analyzes\nand never governs; its candidate SUB-redundant-execution is argued, not\nbound, and reaching the registry is for the board under bylaws 9.16.\n\nOpen questions gains the residential entry. It lands at 9 by the date\norder the file has always kept - 2026-09-08 falls between the sybil\nentry and the transport fee - so the transport fee renumbers to 10.\nNothing outside the file''s own summary paragraph cited entry 9.\n\nConformance suite: RESULT: all executed checks PASS, exit 0.\n\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"2fa6ffaac13cd45725a0141106ebedb5d992411c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/git/trees/2fa6ffaac13cd45725a0141106ebedb5d992411c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/git/commits/fc9feb47d6fa513a06bd3cbeb7b904ee7714b57d","verification":{"payload":"tree 2fa6ffaac13cd45725a0141106ebedb5d992411c\nparent 1eecd004a656076185c2e11dce7eb5b82e354f7d\nauthor the Institute <info@ntari.org> 1789575025 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789575025 -0400\n\nCarry the residential substrate work into the standard (#8)\n\nThe 2026-09-08 board review raised three physical facts about running\nsubstrate on consumer broadband that the document did not address:\ndynamic addresses behind carrier-grade NAT with acceptable-use policies\nbarring inbound servers, asymmetric and capped uplink, and a host\noperator who can read the memory of a machine they physically possess.\nThat work was done in the document store and never reached this\nrepository, so the standard has been silent on the residential case\nwhile the reference protocol was already built for it.\n\nThe document gains one paragraph in the Substrate protocol tier: nodes\njoin the market over encrypted overlays and outbound polling, without\nrequiring open inbound ports or a static address. This is the\nresidential case of line 11 rather than a new provision - a substrate\nthat ran only where a provider permits inbound service would carry a\nchokepoint at every provider, which line 11 forbids. Registered as\nSUB-no-inbound-requirement, implementation-bound, taking the registry\nfrom 25 invariants to 26 and the README counts with it.\n\nThe seven translations carry the same paragraph and move their source\nsnapshot to 2026-09-08. They do not yet carry the transport fee from\n#6; the snapshot date says so rather than implying coverage they lack.\n\nBandwidth and confidential execution are not answered by the document.\nThey are argued in P1-004, published here because open question 9 links\nto it and the link would otherwise dangle - the same reason the concept\ntriage and prior instruments were published in #1. The paper analyzes\nand never governs; its candidate SUB-redundant-execution is argued, not\nbound, and reaching the registry is for the board under bylaws 9.16.\n\nOpen questions gains the residential entry. It lands at 9 by the date\norder the file has always kept - 2026-09-08 falls between the sybil\nentry and the transport fee - so the transport fee renumbers to 10.\nNothing outside the file''s own summary paragraph cited entry 9.\n\nConformance suite: RESULT: all executed checks PASS, exit 0.\n\nSigned-off-by: Jodson Graves <info@ntari.org>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqr9xCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAPE0QAFyQmg84Pt4yokjyvFKaXgyN\nO1OJnuZ8UTifDFdCww7zYqzq1JMurhpJnmcDRGULp9HhQruqUXcvR0U8wSNzXtF9\nYpi1DwmG77SmxiseZsHwELFoKmVzpRLb+7cJFwlEwlVMkYiiyRF93ELbWg8RDTOy\n5hD++P7TMrOUihkjSDA8SyK4MuZYab0Lyx7mjgvmv3C21wUKaPI3ZXspF8GVfLTz\nE8oEtGQ4xG9Y+P7AEMiFxjuCFM72huPoaTPNileVm9Gv9jTyKukUuWg1wuZOSb20\nfmiROH3ao22ezqkvYlcjK8MDWqq6p0PocwSdfrp1bnZHGnAGzNcd4FvvV3jBBfwh\n+sgXivuWs2udsamVO5JPARtMY+7SHxaKMrdaRCMcrBvHqw/QzlT1RBKq4bAt81Po\nmo/yMXAyC6+K8xEmj42bFGxoVSolMLCGaWTUZaW+XjbPTHBP60jIPmARojIDntW/\ntWdIkwqmk+NrgS7DN69xuCQmdC7durvY8MNA7qiMcP5oh1j1PCZu6+BQwhZ7svfT\nV7y+gQURpKKyWdq525WnfXS/iBHGvhyK5+j4LxkTBznnR2c3H/+AxmJQzpFFdUFs\nrR158H57W0t/oYSkKutoQeV4ymIzyprgX8nR2hJt1/cIv9F1TFTkUQT5jSEnEjH9\n4AXpefP8DiXpmabNvzjN\n=oqBK\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-16T16:10:26Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commit/fc9feb47d6fa513a06bd3cbeb7b904ee7714b57d","node_id":"C_kwDOTifuNtoAKGZjOWZlYjQ3ZDZmYTUxM2EwNmJkM2NiZWI3YjkwNGVlNzcxNGI1N2Q","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commit/1eecd004a656076185c2e11dce7eb5b82e354f7d","sha":"1eecd004a656076185c2e11dce7eb5b82e354f7d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/1eecd004a656076185c2e11dce7eb5b82e354f7d"}],"sha":"fc9feb47d6fa513a06bd3cbeb7b904ee7714b57d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/fc9feb47d6fa513a06bd3cbeb7b904ee7714b57d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/196215170?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","id":196215170,"login":"NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","node_id":"U_kgDOC7IBgg","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/1eecd004a656076185c2e11dce7eb5b82e354f7d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-13T11:13:07Z","email":"info@ntari.org","name":"the Institute"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-13T11:13:07Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Gate the document on the conformance suite (#7)\n\nThe suite has been the load-bearing rung since it was published, and nothing\nran it. The repository carried no workflows at all; the checks reporting on a\npull request were CodeQL and DCO, neither of which knows what the document is.\nThat was survivable while amendments arrived as separate proposal files, which\ncould not break the document by construction. Now that amendments edit the\ndocument itself, the suite is the only thing standing between a malformed edit\nand the standard, and it ran only when someone remembered to run it.\n\nAdds the Conformance workflow, which runs the suite on every pull request and\non pushes to main. Deliberately no paths filter: this is meant to become a\nrequired status check, and a required check that never runs leaves a pull\nrequest blocked forever. The suite finishes in well under a second, so running\nit on every pull request costs nothing and always reports.\n\nAdds .githooks/pre-push for the same check locally, installed with\n  git config core.hooksPath .githooks\nand says plainly in both the hook and CONTRIBUTING.md that this is a\nconvenience rather than a gate — hooks are not distributed with a repository,\nand --no-verify skips them. The pull request check is what holds.\n\nAdds CONTRIBUTING.md, which the repository never had despite DCO being enforced\non every commit. It records the sign-off requirement and the fact that DCO\nmatches the trailer against the commit author exactly, which is the failure\nmode contributors actually hit. It also writes down what the suite constrains:\nthe nine sections, no new third-level heading inside a layer, twelve lines, the\ntwenty-five anchors, no product names, no addresses beyond the footer.\n\n.gitattributes pins the hook and the workflow to LF. Checked out with CRLF on a\nLinux runner the hook has a bad interpreter line and will not run.\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"4fd6431dc68ab1a9f9e497e8a1261735d7ac0d41","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/git/trees/4fd6431dc68ab1a9f9e497e8a1261735d7ac0d41"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/git/commits/1eecd004a656076185c2e11dce7eb5b82e354f7d","verification":{"payload":"tree 4fd6431dc68ab1a9f9e497e8a1261735d7ac0d41\nparent b14f760071ee9da4f7c0c5d4a7c9b8c2027520ad\nauthor the Institute <info@ntari.org> 1789297987 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789297987 -0400\n\nGate the document on the conformance suite (#7)\n\nThe suite has been the load-bearing rung since it was published, and nothing\nran it. The repository carried no workflows at all; the checks reporting on a\npull request were CodeQL and DCO, neither of which knows what the document is.\nThat was survivable while amendments arrived as separate proposal files, which\ncould not break the document by construction. Now that amendments edit the\ndocument itself, the suite is the only thing standing between a malformed edit\nand the standard, and it ran only when someone remembered to run it.\n\nAdds the Conformance workflow, which runs the suite on every pull request and\non pushes to main. Deliberately no paths filter: this is meant to become a\nrequired status check, and a required check that never runs leaves a pull\nrequest blocked forever. The suite finishes in well under a second, so running\nit on every pull request costs nothing and always reports.\n\nAdds .githooks/pre-push for the same check locally, installed with\n  git config core.hooksPath .githooks\nand says plainly in both the hook and CONTRIBUTING.md that this is a\nconvenience rather than a gate — hooks are not distributed with a repository,\nand --no-verify skips them. The pull request check is what holds.\n\nAdds CONTRIBUTING.md, which the repository never had despite DCO being enforced\non every commit. It records the sign-off requirement and the fact that DCO\nmatches the trailer against the commit author exactly, which is the failure\nmode contributors actually hit. It also writes down what the suite constrains:\nthe nine sections, no new third-level heading inside a layer, twelve lines, the\ntwenty-five anchors, no product names, no addresses beyond the footer.\n\n.gitattributes pins the hook and the workflow to LF. Checked out with CRLF on a\nLinux runner the hook has a bad interpreter line and will not run.\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqpoVDCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAtOgQAIEM4We5b7wBVrwWYw8ithJW\nMow8pankEgmbejn9MuG3ixZnCB/m9nI2gqjIlTnGCqUoXayq670ArDG9LdkTRNJd\nVZGYf0Ub1MkeTL7Apclbq5EJEAcBtCOhtszLxHQkZLiobHCMRXK9PAiscKzTUU9C\nZJI0uCYkf1EXe3LfYjGrhoILc5cnDLECKggFqXVfVxmfGkpAb/XkrZRZUaZSpnMr\np2G0QxAVVHQYaoGfucR/JtK+DSdZtlDOgmvgNMScZkixU3kxbZmLQ0r162gxce4W\n8aEMYThfCTOcsvVYmWC73foJNd09z1Gl0qL9DLc5z9Zi12Mwpo1mL/8zmblyvsFx\nkvyFY9R9oaIGIzAJSrNIVt/fzTgQ7d3ppWhTtZwoHPen5bqL5qCY644A6WqLeW6V\nXb9QREMO8dvU7ZmWWL7RK6GpgenwzX1GIT+YfYjMNxA74LuXZKKZCN+4tvruH9+m\nAYIFC91tHbHexjpDN90Um2VOZJN6dctPJzobDvpxQX/v+Elmtds3XrfcdxHnDU1K\n+rRaSkxchyfL9BzCdTgx482OY3aEGEYCl1uz2tk3GDJmhzT9HJZ18QOV+t5ZT3oc\nt/wYOA20u5gUmN8P4H+qFf9Xe+LTcDQFyOQ9ACukua0KlSZPqxY75cVmSWW9uEpb\nAKY2yjY56Vzrmxdvmxq5\n=oImn\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-13T11:13:08Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commit/1eecd004a656076185c2e11dce7eb5b82e354f7d","node_id":"C_kwDOTifuNtoAKDFlZWNkMDA0YTY1NjA3NjE4NWMyZTExZGNlN2ViNWI4MmUzNTRmN2Q","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commit/b14f760071ee9da4f7c0c5d4a7c9b8c2027520ad","sha":"b14f760071ee9da4f7c0c5d4a7c9b8c2027520ad","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/b14f760071ee9da4f7c0c5d4a7c9b8c2027520ad"}],"sha":"1eecd004a656076185c2e11dce7eb5b82e354f7d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/1eecd004a656076185c2e11dce7eb5b82e354f7d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/196215170?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","id":196215170,"login":"NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","node_id":"U_kgDOC7IBgg","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/NetworkTheoryAppliedResearchInstitute","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/commits/b14f760071ee9da4f7c0c5d4a7c9b8c2027520ad/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-13T11:13:00Z","email":"info@ntari.org","name":"the Institute"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-13T11:13:00Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Price transport as a third sovereign spend (#6)\n\nThe standard defined cross-community trade as two atomic sovereign spends cited\ninto the Record and said nothing about how the orchestrator carrying those\nspends is paid. Left undefined, transport is unpaid and unsustainable on\nprosumer hardware, paid out-of-band and invisible to covenant discipline, or\nabsorbed by operators — which rebuilds the platform-as-intermediary model the\narchitecture exists to replace.\n\nThe exchange remains two sovereign spends; line 5 is untouched. What grows is\nthe citation that settles it, which now carries a third spend for the carrier''s\nfee, escrowed with the trade at initiation and released jointly. If delivery is\nnot attested inside the offer''s commitment window all three revert, and the\norchestrator''s own attestation does not count toward the threshold releasing\nits own fee. The fee settles in the home ledger of the prosumers whose capacity\nwas carried, so lines 3-5 hold: value stays home and only truth crosses. The\ncredit it earns is held the same six ways and cited by key, per line 7.\n\nTransport becomes a published offer rather than a privileged position, which\ngives line 11 economic teeth — a dominant carrier is undercut rather than\nregulated. Atomicity stays a property of the Record''s citation rule, so\norchestrators may be lossy and retry-based, which is what prosumer hardware\nwith churn, sleep and relay actually is.\n\nFive questions this raises and does not answer are filed as open question 9\nrather than written into the standard as though settled: metered versus fixed\npricing, the split when prosumers come from two communities, whether paid\nwitnesses earn credit under the same rule without recursing, whether the\noperator''s witness slot is mandatory, and the default commitment window.\n\nThe conformance suite reports RESULT: all executed checks PASS, exit 0, with\nall 25 registered anchors still bound to their sections.\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"f923c1ce5aff2a1617044d27e206705bf04d328f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/git/trees/f923c1ce5aff2a1617044d27e206705bf04d328f"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/NTARI-RAND/Janus/git/commits/b14f760071ee9da4f7c0c5d4a7c9b8c2027520ad","verification":{"payload":"tree f923c1ce5aff2a1617044d27e206705bf04d328f\nparent 477bed56a4ae1d668cd06835df3ff47762c13a00\nauthor the Institute <info@ntari.org> 1789297980 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789297980 -0400\n\nPrice transport as a third sovereign spend (#6)\n\nThe standard defined cross-community trade as two atomic sovereign spends cited\ninto the Record and said nothing about how the orchestrator carrying those\nspends is paid. Left undefined, transport is unpaid and unsustainable on\nprosumer hardware, paid out-of-band and invisible to covenant discipline, or\nabsorbed by operators — which rebuilds the platform-as-intermediary model the\narchitecture exists to replace.\n\nThe exchange remains two sovereign spends; line 5 is untouched. What grows is\nthe citation that settles it, which now carries a third spend for the carrier''s\nfee, escrowed with the trade at initiation and released jointly. If delivery is\nnot attested inside the offer''s commitment window all three revert, and the\norchestrator''s own attestation does not count toward the threshold releasing\nits own fee. The fee settles in the home ledger of the prosumers whose capacity\nwas carried, so lines 3-5 hold: value stays home and only truth crosses. The\ncredit it earns is held the same six ways and cited by key, per line 7.\n\nTransport becomes a published offer rather than a privileged position, which\ngives line 11 economic teeth — a dominant carrier is undercut rather than\nregulated. Atomicity stays a property of the Record''s citation rule, so\norchestrators may be lossy and retry-based, which is what prosumer hardware\nwith churn, sleep and relay actually is.\n\nFive questions this raises and does not answer are filed as open question 9\nrather than written into the standard as though settled: metered versus fixed\npricing, the split when prosumers come from two communities, whether paid\nwitnesses earn credit under the same rule without recursing, whether the\noperator''s witness slot is mandatory, and the default commitment window.\n\nThe conformance suite reports RESULT: all executed checks PASS, exit 0, with\nall 25 registered anchors still bound to their sections.\n\nSigned-off-by: the Institute <info@ntari.org>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqpoU8CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAANYIQADBXBDGf+cbWcdgyfEjMudWJ\nhrAzhJTTs/V0e5H9ghT9MzeE/6vE+Hqk2+AkuSSa8+ijhE1D610+ndKwRotHHEa0\nSg+ihce9SymH7CVg/x3AKn0ECsZahl9+AVSSKYzxA7jdctoJF5z0d2l5MidAxfEl\nmHfX9I/gj3PcwcZnbyPr4elqtg6eiYkPKCM/iMR2IIQTcYk8MtXFhC0No4yVVA2I\n